# XSH insque() and remque()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Both are XSI. The element structure has a forward pointer first and a
# backward pointer second. What remque() leaves in the removed element's own
# pointers is not specified and is not checked.

assertion  XSH.insque.001
anchor     functions/insque.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1988
says       insque(&e, NULL) sets both the forward and the backward pointer of e
           to null pointers, whatever they held before.
test       t/001-initial.c

assertion  XSH.insque.002
anchor     functions/insque.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1988
says       insque(&b, &a) with a the last element of a linear queue makes b
           follow a: a's forward pointer is b, b's backward pointer is a, and
           b's forward pointer is null; repeated, a chain of five is linked
           both ways.
test       t/002-append.c

assertion  XSH.insque.003
anchor     functions/insque.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1988
says       insque(&c, &a) with a followed by b puts c immediately after a: the
           queue is a, c, b, with the forward and backward pointers of all
           three consistent.
test       t/003-middle.c

assertion  XSH.insque.004
anchor     functions/insque.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1988
says       remque() of the middle, first and last element of a linear queue
           of four links the remaining neighbours to each other in both
           directions and leaves the ends null (the first element's backward
           pointer, the last element's forward pointer).
test       t/004-remove.c

assertion  XSH.insque.005
anchor     functions/insque.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1988
says       A circular queue starts with one element whose forward and
           backward pointers are its own address; insque() after it gives a
           ring of two, insque() after the first again a ring of three in the
           order first, new, old, and remque() of an element closes the ring
           over the rest.
test       t/005-circular.c
